# NET D cell — Set-HOF `filter` RESULT-SET CTOR, key-channel axis.
#
# AXIS CELL: element type = `@derive(Hashable)` struct, PRIMITIVES ONLY, members SHARING their first 8 bytes  ·
# destination ordered-ness = UNORDERED `HashSet`  ·
# KEY CHANNEL × KEY WIDTH = same, the unordered arm
#
# ⚠ THE MECHANISM IS KEY CHANNEL × KEY WIDTH, NOT "element type". Before the
# fix `try_lower_set_hof`'s filter arm minted the result set with the RAW
# `gorget_(ordered_)set_new` symbol and a HARDCODED `OpConstI64(8)`: a fixed
# 8-byte key with no `hash_fn`/`eq_fn` bridge, i.e. FNV-1a + `memcmp` over 8
# bytes. That width is ACCIDENTALLY RIGHT for `int64_t`, too WIDE for `bool`,
# and too NARROW for a 16-byte struct whose discriminating field sits past
# byte 8 — which is why `Set[int]` and a `Pt` whose members differ in the
# FIRST 8 bytes are green at HEAD and prove nothing. Do NOT "simplify" this
# cell back into either of those shapes; it would silence the axis.
#
# The fix mints through ONE producer — `set_ctor_name(elem, ordered)` in
# `lower_types.gg`, beside `collection_accumulator_tid` — which emits the
# sized, element-keyed magic name `__gorget_(ordered_)set_new_sized_<T>`;
# `lir_codegen.gg::emit_typed_set_ctor` expands it into BOTH the key width
# and the `hash_fn`/`eq_fn`/`key_drop`/`key_clone` table. Rust gg mints the
# same result via `gorget_set_new_like`.
#
# ⚠ The tree's precedent test `set_literal_selects_key_channel_by_element_type`
# says an OUTPUT assertion on the all-primitive member is vacuous for the
# LITERAL path and pins it by emitted symbols instead. For the HOF path an
# output assertion DOES work, because the defect there is the key WIDTH, not
# only the missing bridge — hence `.contains()` read-backs below.
#
# RED-VERIFIED (self-host lane, pre-fix driver): `1` where `2` is correct (two distinct members collapsed into one)
#
# FIXTURE PLACEMENT: subdirectory (see any NET A cell's header for the full
# reasoning).  LANE: self-host only (Core #9 implementation-internal
# exemption; the Rust column is what the `.expect` records).  STAGE: 0.
# RUNG: 2 — no constructible held-fixed control for a Set-HOF receiver; the
# expected value is the Rust lane's, hand-checked against the definition
# (`filter` preserves the receiver's element type and ordered-ness, so the
# result must contain exactly the members satisfying the predicate).

from std.collections import HashSet

@derive(Hashable, Equatable)
struct Pt:
    int x
    int y

void main():
    HashSet[Pt] s = HashSet[Pt]()
    int i = 0
    while i < 3:
        s.add(Pt(0, i))
        i += 1
    HashSet[Pt] keep = s.filter((Pt p): p.y != 1)
    print(f"{keep.len()}")
    if keep.contains(Pt(0, 0)):
        print("HAS-a")
    else:
        print("MISS-a")
    if keep.contains(Pt(0, 2)):
        print("HAS-b")
    else:
        print("MISS-b")
